WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.620
Bearer tokens grant access to whoever presents them, so storage, transmission, logging, and lifetime matter.

2
00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:14.200
A Node.js API that receives an Authorization header should not treat the token as trusted just because it has the right shape.

3
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:28.300
The request pipeline should validate the token using trusted configuration, map validated claims to an account or service identity, check account state, and pass only a verified identity to later authorization logic.

4
00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:32.660
JWTs are signed data structures, not magic security containers.

5
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:44.680
Before trusting JWT claims, an API should validate the signature, expected algorithm, issuer, audience, expiration, not-before time if used, and intended use.

6
00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:47.020
Decoding a token is not validation.

7
00:00:47.120 --> 00:01:00.400
Accepting unsigned tokens, unexpected algorithms, stale keys, wrong issuers, wrong audiences, or claims from the wrong token type can break the boundary between identity assertion and application trust.

8
00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:09.400
Token lifetime, refresh behavior, revocation, key rotation, and compromised-token response should be designed intentionally.

9
00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:13.820
Long-lived tokens reduce friction but increase exposure when leaked.

10
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.720
Short-lived tokens reduce exposure but require safe refresh patterns.

11
00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:28.500
Sensitive tokens should not be logged, placed in URLs, committed to repositories, copied into support tickets, or exposed to browser scripts unnecessarily.

12
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:35.800
API keys can identify an integration or application, but they do not automatically prove an individual user's identity.
